WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.332 --> 00:00:09.292
And let's move on to
something a little bit more

00:00:09.292 --> 00:00:11.602
exciting like an
app with a plot.

00:00:11.602 --> 00:00:16.072
So this application
here is pretty similar,

00:00:16.072 --> 00:00:19.072
but now, when I
change this slider,

00:00:19.072 --> 00:00:22.522
it's going to change, I think,
the number of bins that go

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:24.392
into computing this histogram.

00:00:24.392 --> 00:00:28.641
So you can see as I
increase the number of bins,

00:00:28.641 --> 00:00:31.582
this histogram is going to
have more stagger to it.

00:00:31.582 --> 00:00:37.772
So yeah, this one has a slightly
more complex user interface.

00:00:37.772 --> 00:00:40.792
And notice as I make
the window wider,

00:00:40.792 --> 00:00:43.522
this actually looks a little
bit more like a sidebar

00:00:43.522 --> 00:00:50.402
than it does an input panel
or something like that.

00:00:50.402 --> 00:00:56.812
So this sort of functionality is
provided by this layout sidebar

00:00:56.812 --> 00:00:59.192
from the UI subpackage.

00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:02.482
So with the layout
sidebar, it really

00:01:02.482 --> 00:01:07.762
wants a sidebar panel
and a main panel.

00:01:07.762 --> 00:01:10.882
And so on the
lefthand side here,

00:01:10.882 --> 00:01:12.742
I have the slider,
similar to what

00:01:12.742 --> 00:01:16.852
we had before, another
output text verbatim

00:01:16.852 --> 00:01:19.822
on the righthand side,
and the main panel

00:01:19.822 --> 00:01:21.952
and then a plot underneath that.

00:01:21.952 --> 00:01:27.022
And this example starts to
introduce some reactivity

00:01:27.022 --> 00:01:29.312
concepts.

00:01:29.312 --> 00:01:33.172
So with this app's
reactive.calc,

00:01:33.172 --> 00:01:40.522
this is a decorator for
defining a calculation that

00:01:40.522 --> 00:01:43.762
depends on some input
values and then returns

00:01:43.762 --> 00:01:49.232
another value from some
combination of input values.

00:01:49.232 --> 00:01:52.882
So this is overly
simplistic example

00:01:52.882 --> 00:01:57.622
that just reads
the slider value,

00:01:57.622 --> 00:02:01.612
and then multiplies
it by two, and gives

00:02:01.612 --> 00:02:04.312
this reactive
calculation a name of R.

00:02:04.312 --> 00:02:06.622
So once I have this
function defined

00:02:06.622 --> 00:02:09.112
as a reactive
calculation, then I

00:02:09.112 --> 00:02:13.372
can read it just like I
would read an input value

00:02:13.372 --> 00:02:16.462
inside of another
reactive context,

00:02:16.462 --> 00:02:19.477
like this rendered text output.

00:02:22.952 --> 00:02:29.762
So this is-- for all
practical purposes,

00:02:29.762 --> 00:02:35.272
this is essentially the same
as just putting this expression

00:02:35.272 --> 00:02:44.662
into the read of R, but for
more sophisticated reactive web

00:02:44.662 --> 00:02:48.142
applications, this is a
really useful construct

00:02:48.142 --> 00:02:53.752
for encapsulating a chunk
of a reactive calculation.

00:02:53.752 --> 00:02:58.312
Whether it's performing
a data summary

00:02:58.312 --> 00:03:03.832
or computing a model
from some data,

00:03:03.832 --> 00:03:07.732
it's always a good
idea to put those steps

00:03:07.732 --> 00:03:09.802
into reactive calculations.

00:03:09.802 --> 00:03:12.832
Basically, it gives
Shiny more information

00:03:12.832 --> 00:03:17.462
to do less work when it
makes sense to do less work.

00:03:17.462 --> 00:03:20.992
So if you have a big-- you
can define a big reactive

00:03:20.992 --> 00:03:25.832
chain with several calculations
that depend on each other

00:03:25.832 --> 00:03:30.532
and if one of those
calculations gets

00:03:30.532 --> 00:03:32.902
invalidated because the
value is out of date

00:03:32.902 --> 00:03:36.082
for whatever reason,
Shiny knows to not go back

00:03:36.082 --> 00:03:39.622
all the way to the top of that
graph and recompute everything.

00:03:39.622 --> 00:03:44.122
It can just say I just need to
do the last two calculations

00:03:44.122 --> 00:03:49.402
and then knows to do less
work when it makes sense to.

00:03:49.402 --> 00:03:53.582
That is reactive
calculations in a nutshell.

00:03:53.582 --> 00:03:57.652
And then to start showing
you some different variations

00:03:57.652 --> 00:04:02.362
on rendering different types
of objects into UI, here's

00:04:02.362 --> 00:04:04.492
an at.render plot.

00:04:04.492 --> 00:04:07.342
And these decorators
can actually

00:04:07.342 --> 00:04:11.002
be used without parentheses
or with parentheses.

00:04:11.002 --> 00:04:13.221
You can call it a function.

00:04:13.221 --> 00:04:15.952
And that's particularly
useful for something

00:04:15.952 --> 00:04:18.802
like render plot, which
has additional arguments

00:04:18.802 --> 00:04:23.392
that you can specify some
alt text to put on this image

00:04:23.392 --> 00:04:24.922
to make it more accessible.

00:04:24.922 --> 00:04:30.022
Then you use it just like
how you would a render text.

00:04:30.022 --> 00:04:33.382
You define a function, and
you can give this function

00:04:33.382 --> 00:04:39.212
a name, which should match
an ID in the user interface.

00:04:39.212 --> 00:04:43.792
And then inside of this
function definition,

00:04:43.792 --> 00:04:46.672
I can do something like
use that Matplotlib

00:04:46.672 --> 00:04:52.012
to create a histogram
where the number of bins

00:04:52.012 --> 00:04:56.152
in this histogram comes from
the value of the slider.

00:04:56.152 --> 00:04:59.602
At least currently, the
way render plot works,

00:04:59.602 --> 00:05:03.592
you're going to want to always
return a Matplotlib object.

00:05:03.592 --> 00:05:06.052
And then we'll
know how to render

00:05:06.052 --> 00:05:07.897
that object into an image.

00:05:11.212 --> 00:05:15.172
I do also want to mention this
render plot has the ability

00:05:15.172 --> 00:05:19.912
to understand more than
just Matplotlib objects,

00:05:19.912 --> 00:05:25.912
and I've actually whipped
up this example app

00:05:25.912 --> 00:05:27.302
to demonstrate that.

00:05:27.302 --> 00:05:32.242
So I've at least laid out
a few different packages

00:05:32.242 --> 00:05:35.002
here, but this is not--

00:05:35.002 --> 00:05:38.722
render plot is not limited
to just these packages.

00:05:38.722 --> 00:05:46.762
Essentially, any package that
builds on Matplotlib or pill

00:05:46.762 --> 00:05:50.217
will be supported
through render plot.

00:05:50.217 --> 00:05:52.342
A lot of the-- what a lot
of these packages will do

00:05:52.342 --> 00:05:55.072
will essentially build
on that Matplotlib,

00:05:55.072 --> 00:06:00.052
like Plotnine, which actually
gives more like a ggplot2 type

00:06:00.052 --> 00:06:01.972
interface to creating graphics.

00:06:01.972 --> 00:06:06.422
And they'll essentially
subclass a Matplotlib object,

00:06:06.422 --> 00:06:12.902
meaning they are a special
form of a Matplotlib object.

00:06:12.902 --> 00:06:17.182
So as long as your
object subclasses

00:06:17.182 --> 00:06:20.542
Matplotlib in some
way, render plot

00:06:20.542 --> 00:06:22.712
should be able to handle it.

00:06:22.712 --> 00:06:29.422
So here's a Plotnine graph
in the Shiny application

00:06:29.422 --> 00:06:32.572
I've programmed to allow
you to change x variable, y

00:06:32.572 --> 00:06:36.112
variable, and color.

00:06:36.112 --> 00:06:38.002
We also support Seaborn.

00:06:40.522 --> 00:06:44.272
You might be familiar with
Pandas' plotting interface,

00:06:44.272 --> 00:06:47.782
where you can essentially,
from your data frame object,

00:06:47.782 --> 00:06:52.432
call .plot on that
data frame object.

00:06:52.432 --> 00:06:56.932
And that will return, I think
in every case, a Matplotlib

00:06:56.932 --> 00:06:57.532
object.

00:06:57.532 --> 00:07:02.152
So you can just basically call
.plot and return that value,

00:07:02.152 --> 00:07:05.302
and render plot
should handle that.

00:07:05.302 --> 00:07:10.172
There's also Hollowvies,
which supports all different--

00:07:10.172 --> 00:07:14.332
it also has interactive
visualization capabilities,

00:07:14.332 --> 00:07:17.827
but you can also use
Matplotlib with Hollowvies.

00:07:20.332 --> 00:07:26.602
xarray and geopandas, so
lots of different packages

00:07:26.602 --> 00:07:31.132
out there that luckily
build on top of Matplotlib,

00:07:31.132 --> 00:07:34.552
and you can all use
with render plot.

00:07:34.552 --> 00:07:39.482
So just to give you a quick
idea of what I mean there,

00:07:39.482 --> 00:07:44.602
so this is actually the source
code for that application that

00:07:44.602 --> 00:07:46.232
were are looking at.

00:07:46.232 --> 00:07:48.472
So here's a render
plot with Seaborn

00:07:48.472 --> 00:07:53.122
that's creating the scatterplot
with the kernel density

00:07:53.122 --> 00:07:56.332
estimate on top of it.

00:07:56.332 --> 00:08:00.832
Plotnine was the more
ggplot2-like interface here,

00:08:00.832 --> 00:08:05.032
where I'm reading like an
x input value and a y input

00:08:05.032 --> 00:08:06.422
value.

00:08:06.422 --> 00:08:09.152
So whenever those
change, Shiny will know

00:08:09.152 --> 00:08:12.247
to re-execute this function.

00:08:15.292 --> 00:08:18.482
As I mentioned with
pandas, there's a dot plot.

00:08:18.482 --> 00:08:21.362
So you can call that and
return whatever that returns.

00:08:23.962 --> 00:08:27.152
Hollowviews, it's a little
bit less straightforward.

00:08:27.152 --> 00:08:31.882
There's this render function
from Hollowviews that

00:08:31.882 --> 00:08:33.652
takes in a back end argument.

00:08:33.652 --> 00:08:36.772
So as long as you specify
I want to use Matplotlib,

00:08:36.772 --> 00:08:38.332
then you can use it
with render plot.

00:08:41.092 --> 00:08:44.512
xarray is similar to
pandas in the sense

00:08:44.512 --> 00:08:50.782
that it has like a
similar dot plot-ike

00:08:50.782 --> 00:08:56.282
interface from its
special data structure.

00:08:56.282 --> 00:09:00.802
And that also returns
Matplotlib objects.

00:09:00.802 --> 00:09:03.652
Again, geopandas,
a lot like pandas,

00:09:03.652 --> 00:09:05.468
where you can just call .plot.

00:09:08.722 --> 00:09:14.632
And then missingno, I think,
also returns Matplotlib object,

00:09:14.632 --> 00:09:18.012
this matrix function here.

